### Example usage

```tsx
import CheckoutPage from './CheckoutPage';
import { CartItem, DeliveryAddress } from './types';

const sampleCartItems: CartItem[] = [
  { id: '1', name: 'Wireless Headphones', unitPrice: 79.99, quantity: 1 },
  { id: '2', name: 'USB-C Cable (2m)', unitPrice: 12.5, quantity: 2 },
];

function CheckoutContainer() {
  return (
    <CheckoutPage
      cartItems={sampleCartItems}
      onBackToCart={() => console.log('navigate back to cart')}
      onPay={async (address: DeliveryAddress) => {
        console.log('Paying, deliver to:', address);
        // call your payment API here
      }}
    />
  );
}

export default CheckoutContainer;
```

Notes:
- `CheckoutPage` is intentionally dumb about navigation/payment — `onBackToCart` and `onPay` are injected so you can wire in your router and payment provider.
- Validation is basic (required fields, loose postal code/phone patterns) — tighten it to match your real address/payment requirements.
- Responsive breakpoint at 720px stacks the two columns; at 480px the address form's side-by-side fields stack too.
